    "reading": {
        "body": "New Mexico's 2026 legislative session produced 36 tracked bills, and the ones that passed are not about AI.\n\nThe bills that cleared the legislature are appropriations and health licensure compacts: SB240, HB2, HB50, and SB1. Every bill with artificial intelligence in its title, including the Artificial Intelligence Accountability Act, the Artificial Intelligence Government Use Act, the Chatbot Safety Act, and the Artificial Intelligence Transparency Act, sits at Introduced. The same is true of the data and privacy measures, SB192 and HB214, and of the Voice and Visual Likeness Rights Act. The one AI-adjacent item that did move, HB330 on AI ethics as a school science elective, is also only Introduced.\n\nThe enforcement activity is running ahead of the statute. A jury found Facebook deceived users over data privacy on September 27, and the state is seeking up to $40 billion from Meta. The attorney general and a state lawmaker have announced a push to rein in rogue AI models. That is litigation and press conference, not enacted law.\n\nFor compute and power, the data offers one signal and it is federal rather than state. Senator Heinrich's GRID Savings Act would force AI data centers to pay for grid upgrades. New Mexico's own bills include a quantum facility infrastructure tax credit and two water measures concerning Taos, but nothing that directly addresses data center load or interconnection. A reader should not conclude from this data that New Mexico has settled its approach to AI, to data centers, or to who pays for the power they draw. It shows a state where the proposals exist and the binding decisions have not been made.",
